---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/relative-humidity-static-plastic-film';
const title = 'Relative Humidity and Static on Plastic Film: Read Temperature Too';
const seoTitle = 'Humidity and Static on Film: Read RH with Temperature';
const description = 'Read relative humidity with air temperature when investigating static on plastic film. Separate ambient observations, equipment limits and process acceptance.';
const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What else should I record with RH?',
    a: 'Air temperature, measured at the same time and location.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Does higher humidity prove static control is adequate?',
    a: 'No. Assess the electrical observations and applicable process criteria; humidity alone does not verify control performance.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is an ionizer humidity range a process target?',
    a: 'No. Separate equipment operating conditions, test conditions and the requirements used to accept your film process.'
  }
];
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/humidity-static-context-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/humidity-static-context-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-10-18', dateModified: '2026-10-18', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqItems.map(item => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a } }))
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Humidity and Static on Film</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Static Control</span><span class="blog-tag">Film Processing</span><span class="blog-tag">Environmental Context</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">Suppose two shift notes show matching relative-humidity percentages but different static readings. Before deciding that humidity helped or failed, read the temperature, material and measurement context. An environmental record can help frame the investigation; it cannot supply the missing electrical result.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>October 18, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>4 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/humidity-static-context-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/humidity-static-context-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/humidity-static-context-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/humidity-static-context-v1.jpg" alt="Conceptual record guide pairing air RH and temperature with separate material and process notes and electrical instrument, quantity, reading and setup. No measured values." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original conceptual illustration. Three complementary records; no test results or humidity-to-charge conversion.</em></p>

      <section id="rh-temperature">
        <h2>Read RH with the air temperature</h2>
        <p><a href="https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=HUMIDITY" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA's National Weather Service</a> explains that RH depends on both atmospheric moisture and temperature. A matching percentage at different temperatures does not establish matching moisture content. Record RH and air temperature together, with a timestamp and sensor location.</p>
        <p>The air sensor does not directly measure water in the film or its electrical state.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="research-context">
        <h2>Check which humidity variable a study uses</h2>
        <p>In <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.04345" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Xu and Grosshans' 2022 author manuscript</a>, the charge-to-mass ratio of PMMA particles conveyed through a duct rose and then fell as <strong>absolute humidity</strong> increased. That result concerns particles under the apparatus's conditions; it is not a relative-humidity rule for moving film. Use it as a reminder to identify the plotted variable and experimental context, not as a prediction for your line.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="paired-records">
        <h2>Matching RH does not make two observations equivalent</h2>
        <p>The following pairs are hypothetical reading examples, not factory trial results or a prescribed experiment. Each exposes a different gap in a comparison.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Illustrative comparisons of humidity, material context and electrical observations">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Illustrative record pair</th><th scope="col">What it establishes</th><th scope="col">What it does not establish</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Same RH; different air temperatures.</th><td>Only the RH percentages match.</td><td>Equal atmospheric moisture or a common electrical state.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Same RH and temperature; different film faces or contact/separation states.</th><td>The recorded RH/temperature pair matches; the material/process context differs.</td><td>That humidity caused an observed difference.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Same recorded RH/temperature pair; different instrument modes or measurement geometry.</th><td>The electrical records were taken with different setups.</td><td>A like-for-like comparison of the electrical results.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>A later lower reading may be useful evidence to investigate, but these mixed comparisons do not identify a single cause. Preserve the observations and leave the cause unresolved.</p>
        <p>When a shift note says “humidity normal,” ask which sensor reading and approved requirement it refers to. Leave unrecorded details marked unknown rather than filling them from another observation.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="three-humidity-requirements">
        <h2>Separate operating limits, test conditions and acceptance</h2>
        <p>A humidity figure can serve different purposes. Keep its label and source document attached:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Equipment operating conditions</strong> describe the environment in which the manufacturer permits a device to operate.</li>
          <li><strong>Test conditions</strong> describe the environment used for a specified measurement.</li>
          <li><strong>Process acceptance criteria</strong> define what the responsible process owner requires for the application.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>An operating range is not itself a film-charge limit. Read product specifications as equipment information; they do not establish a universal production RH target.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="humidity-control-guidance">
        <h2>Keep a humidity requirement tied to its purpose</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://www.esda.org/assets/Documents/e280ccf3ff/Humidity-FAQ-final.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EOS/ESD Association's Humidity FAQ</a> explains that the ANSI/ESD S20.20 framework it discusses does not rely on humidity control. If a program specifies humidity conditions, the FAQ calls for monitoring, objective evidence and a response when conditions leave the specified range. This is ESD-program guidance, not a plastic-film process specification.</p>
        <p>Treat a site humidity limit as a documented requirement with a stated basis. A room reading alone does not justify changing or disabling existing static controls.</p>
        <p>For the electrical terms, see our guide to <a href="/blog/surface-resistance-vs-surface-voltage">film resistance and static voltage</a>. If readings change between line positions, the <a href="/blog/static-returns-after-ionization">static-return article</a> addresses that separate investigation.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="faq"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq-list">{faqItems.map(item => <details class="faq-item"><summary>{item.q}</summary><p>{item.a}</p></details>)}</div></section>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Discuss humidity in your film-line review</h2>
        <p>Share the paired air readings, material and process context, and electrical records. Our team can discuss the application and the information needed for an equipment review.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/static-eliminator/st-g-series">Explore ST-G ionizing bars &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Film%20Humidity%20and%20Static%20Review">Discuss your film-line observations &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
